-- title:
--   p. 2724 — the particle system (4.2) is well posed
-- statement:
--   This is the well-posedness of the $N$-particle system (4.2) stated in §4 of Carmona and Delarue.
--
--   Throughout, assumptions (A.1)–(A.7) are in force with constants $\lambda, c_L > 0$. Let $(X_t, Y_t, Z_t)_{0\le t\le T}$ be a solution of the McKean–Vlasov FBSDE (3.1) on a probability space $(\Omega_0, \mathbb P_0)$ carrying an $m$-dimensional Brownian motion, let $\mu_t = \mathbb P_{X_t}$ be its flow of marginal laws, and let $u : [0,T]\times\mathbb R^d \to \mathbb R^d$ be the associated FBSDE value function: for some $c \ge 0$, $|u(t,x)| \le c(1+|x|)$ and $|u(t,x)-u(t,x')| \le c|x-x'|$ for all $t \in [0,T]$, $x, x'$ (this is (3.3)), and $\mathbb P_0$-a.s., $Y_t = u(t, X_t)$ for all $t \in [0,T]$. Let $J$ be the optimal cost of the limiting mean-field problem (4.1),
--   $$J = \mathbb E\Big[g(X_T,\mu_T) + \int_0^T f\big(t, X_t, \mu_t, \hat\alpha(t, X_t, \mu_t, Y_t)\big)\,dt\Big].$$
--   On a probability space $(\Omega, \mathbb P)$ fix a sequence $(W^i)_{i\ge1}$ of independent $m$-dimensional Brownian motions. For $N \ge 1$, $(X^1,\dots,X^N)$ denotes a solution of the particle system (4.2),
--   $$dX^i_t = b\big(t, X^i_t, \bar\mu^N_t, \hat\alpha(t, X^i_t, \mu_t, u(t, X^i_t))\big)dt + \sigma\,dW^i_t,\quad X^i_0 = x_0,\quad \bar\mu^N_t = \frac1N\sum_{j=1}^N \delta_{X^j_t},$$
--   and $\bar\alpha^{N,i}_t = \hat\alpha(t, X^i_t, \mu_t, u(t, X^i_t))$ are the strategies (4.3). For a strategy profile $(\beta^1,\dots,\beta^N)$, $U^i$ are the states (4.5), $dU^i_t = b(t, U^i_t, \bar\nu^N_t, \beta^i_t)dt + \sigma dW^i_t$, $U^i_0 = x_0$, $\bar\nu^N_t = \frac1N\sum_j\delta_{U^j_t}$, and $\bar J^{N,i}(\beta^1,\dots,\beta^N) = \mathbb E[g(U^i_T, \bar\nu^N_T) + \int_0^T f(t, U^i_t, \bar\nu^N_t, \beta^i_t)dt]$ is the cost (4.6) to player $i$. Strategies are progressively measurable for the filtration generated by $(W^1,\dots,W^N)$.
--
--   **Statement.** For every $N\ge1$ the system (4.2) has a solution $(X^1,\dots,X^N)$ on $(\Omega,\mathbb P)$, and any two solutions are indistinguishable: almost surely, $X^i_t = X'^i_t$ for all $t\in[0,T]$ and all $i$.
--
--   The paper justifies it by the regularity (3.3) of $u$ and the Lipschitz continuity and linear growth of $\hat\alpha(t,x,\mu_t,y)$ in $(x,y)$ (Lemma 2.1). The strategies (4.3) of Theorem 4.2 are defined from this solution.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The model, (A.1)–(A.7), $\hat\alpha$ and (3.1) are those of `ProbMFG.Nash.Model` and `ProbMFG.Nash.MFGSolution`; the game objects are those of `ProbMFG.Nash.Game`. Players are indexed by `Fin N`, so the paper's player $1$ is index $0$ and "$2 \le i \le N$" is "$i \ne 0$". The mean-field solution and the game live on two probability spaces; only $\mu$, $J$ and $u$ pass from the first to the second (the paper's "for convenience, we fix a sequence … of independent Brownian motions"). Beyond the page, the hypotheses include: $u$ jointly measurable in $(t,x)$ (the value function $u^\mu$ of Lemma 3.5 is measurable), and Borel measurability of $f$ and $g$ on $[0,T]\times\mathbb R^d\times\mathcal P_2\times\mathbb R^k$ and $\mathbb R^d\times\mathcal P_2$ (part of `Assumptions`), so that the expected costs are expectations of measurable integrands. Solutions of the SDEs are Itô processes in Peng's sense with almost surely continuous paths on $[0,T]$. All norms are Euclidean. Expectations of nonnegative quantities are lower Lebesgue integrals in $[0,\infty]$; costs are real expectations.
-- source:
--   Carmona and Delarue, Probabilistic analysis of mean-field games, SIAM J. Control Optim. 51 (2013), p. 2724, the sentence after (4.2)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ProbMFG_Nash_Game

open MeasureTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace ProbMFG.Nash

/-- p. 2724: the particle system (4.2) is well posed: for every `N ≥ 1` it has a solution, and any two solutions are indistinguishable on `[0, T]`. -/
theorem particle_system_well_posed {d m k : ℕ} (M : Model d m k) (lam cL : ℝ) (hA : Assumptions M lam cL)
    {Ω₀ : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω₀] (P₀ : Measure Ω₀) [IsProbabilityMeasure P₀]
    (W₀ : ℝ≥0 → Ω₀ → Fin m → ℝ) (hW₀ : Peng1990.SMP.IsStdBrownian P₀ W₀)
    (X Y : ℝ≥0 → Ω₀ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (Z : Fin m → ℝ≥0 → Ω₀ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (hXYZ : SolvesMKV M P₀ hW₀ X Y Z)
    (u : ℝ≥0 → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
    (hu : ∃ c ≥ (0 : ℝ), ∀ t ≤ M.T, ∀ x x' : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d),
      ‖u t x‖ ≤ c * (1 + ‖x‖) ∧ ‖u t x - u t x'‖ ≤ c * ‖x - x'‖)
    (hu_meas : Measurable (fun p : ℝ≥0 × EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d) => u p.1 p.2))
    (hYu : ∀ᵐ ω ∂P₀, ∀ t ≤ M.T, Y t ω = u t (X t ω))
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (Ws : ℕ → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Fin m → ℝ) (hWs : ∀ i, Peng1990.SMP.IsStdBrownian P (Ws i))
    (hind : ProbabilityTheory.iIndepFun (fun i ω t => Ws i t ω) P) :
    ∀ N : ℕ, 1 ≤ N →
      (∃ Xs : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d), IsParticleSystem M P hWs (lawFlow P₀ X) u N Xs) ∧
      ∀ Xs Xs' : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d), IsParticleSystem M P hWs (lawFlow P₀ X) u N Xs → IsParticleSystem M P hWs (lawFlow P₀ X) u N Xs' →
        ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t ≤ M.T, ∀ i, Xs i t ω = Xs' i t ω := by sorry

end ProbMFG.Nash
